A||||28||BBBAD GT||Guess the RWHP on my V8 Fiero||||1||
Z||000000||BBBAD GT||08-08-2000||12:39 PM||||It looks like 1FST2M6 (Travis) is going to hook me up with a dyno session at <A HREF="http://www.engineered.net" TARGET=_blank>www.engineered.net</A>  on Sat, right after I give Chris the money for the car.  Here is the motor:<P>Stock 350 longblock from a 86-88 police caprice (I think)<BR>Stock cam<BR>Weiand X celerator single plane manifold<BR>Edelbrock 600 Carb<P>Compression is sort of a mystery, since all cop cars with 350's from 86-88 had the 8.5:1 compression from what I've read.  Chris says the pistons are flat tops, with 4 valve reliefs.  He's calculated it being between 9-9.5:1, if the heads (casting 193 with 1.94/1.50 valves) are between 62-72 cc's, which is good for a street motor.  He's seen the low compression 350 pistons before, and they are dished in a major way.  So, I tend to believe Chris and go with the low 9:1.	<P>My guess will be between 200-215 RWHP, which if you take into consideration the 17% driveline loss, that would be between 235-250 flywheel HP.  Travis said he'll bring a digital camera that takes movies along with pics, so maybe we can post some up for everybody.  Should be a fun day.<P>Any guesses?<P>Also, for all you carbed 350 Fieros out there, what combo are you running? At first, I thought the single plane manifold was too much for a street car, especially with the 600cfm carb, but Chris says the combo works extremely well with the gearing in the Isuzu 5 speed.  He's at the max jets with the 600, so we'll see if a 750 isn't in my future. I just need to drive it to see how it works.<P><BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/BBBAD_GT_SIG.gif">  <P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/Fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD GT</A><BR><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD 5.0</A>||||reg||1
Z||000001||b lo 0||08-08-2000||01:24 PM||||What time?  I'm in Gwinnett County and I wanna go see (and hear) your car!!!  Travis, next time waen me before you invite a big bad V8 Fiero to the ATL area!  That way I won't humiliate myself tring to race them!<P>------------------<BR>'85 GT (in progress)  <BR>titled ICE-plates B LO 0 <BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.angelfire.com/ga3/audioimagery/ice.gif">     <BR>Go to <A HREF="http://www.audioimagery.i-p.com" TARGET=_blank>www.audioimagery.i-p.com</A>  !!!<P><A HREF="http://clubs.excite.com/welcome/main?cid=.zXb1DJ1lsCL&aid=22&auth=" TARGET=_blank>Pontiac Fiero Owner's Club</A>-Pics, Videos and More-Or Post your Own||||reg||1
Z||000002||mrfixit58||08-08-2000||01:45 PM||||BBBAD,<P>You said: "At first, I thought the single plane manifold was too much for a street car, especially with the 600cfm carb, but Chris says the combo works extremely well with the gearing in the Isuzu 5 speed. He's at the max jets with the 600, so we'll see if a 750 isn't in my future. I just need to drive it to see how it works."<P>From experience, you'll get more bottom end torque from a duel plane manifold and a 650 CFM carb. A 750 CFM is too much carb for a stock SBC. If you design your motor to make HP above 2500 with a radical cam and big valves and exhaust, a 750 and single plane will work well. For street type RPM, even if you eventually go with 2.02 valve heads and large lift cam, you best bet is still duel plane and a 650 CFM carb.<P>I built a mild 300 HP street motor for my buddy's SBC 68 Camaro. He bought a brand new 750 Holley for it and removed the "temporary" Rochester from the "performer" aluminum intake. The car ran like crap until it hit about 2600 RPM. I tried jetting it down but it still had no bottom end torque. <P>Since the only change we made was carburation, that had to be the problem. He bought another new Holley, this time a 650 CFM(We used the table on the side of the 750 CFM Holley box). What a difference. The car now screems.<P>Also, other then gas milage and/or acceleration, the transmission should have very little to do with how the motor performs. Tune your motor for the camshaft and heads and get the most of what you have. Don't expect a larger carb to make it a 12 second car unless everything else is designed to do 12s.<P>My $0.02 worth,<BR>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
